3$ decimal places?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073620 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b0df0b9-3484-5408-aadc-01e756c43de7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073620 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the argument of the sine is given in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\sin(0.3) = 0.295\\,520\\ldots \\approx 0.296\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073621 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073621 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$ by converting the angle to degrees." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073621 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9829363-8cde-55fb-a1de-b6460ef451a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073621 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi} 6\\right) \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \\dfrac {180^\\circ} {6} = 30^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\sin 30^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\sin 30^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)  = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073622 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073622 #edb/ref 1071 "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\arcsin(0.75)$ in radians?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073622 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55d74eac-00aa-54cc-8e1f-743e053cc599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073622 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\arcsin(0.75) = 0.848\\,062\\ldots \\approx 0.848 \\, \\textrm{radians}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073623 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073623 #edb/ref 1071 "Is the following statement  true or false?\n\n$$\n\\sin30^\\circ \\:\\overset{\\large\\color{red}?}{=}\\: \\sin \\left(  \\dfrac16 \\right).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073623 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7eadc0f5-b50a-5e8e-9706-7f4fe0554abf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073623 #edb/ref 1074 "The statement is false. Converting $30^\\circ$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n30^\\circ= 30\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{180} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\, \\textrm{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\neq \\dfrac{1}6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073680 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073680 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073680 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073680 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073680 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073680 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073680 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073680 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073680 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073680 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c16dc76e-e756-5f66-9453-ba88b3f37f67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073681 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073681 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073681 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073681 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073681 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio of an Acute Special Angle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073681 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073681 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3a2e44aa-d511-5bb4-bfe3-3213d7df0f1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073682 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074256 #edb/ref 17592